# Organ Trio Sonata No. 4 in E minor, BWV 528: I. Adagio, Vivace
# (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: a trio for two manuals and pedal, three equal lines. Bach
# took the movement from the sinfonia that opens Part II of the cantata
# "Die Himmel erzaehlen die Ehre Gottes", BWV 76 (oboe d'amore, viola da
# gamba and continuo there). A short, grave Adagio in 4/4 ends on a half
# cadence on B; the Vivace in 3/4 is a lively imitative trio in which the
# two upper lines pass the same subject between them over a walking
# pedal.
#
# Bars: Mutopia SonataIV.mid, checked against the LilyPond source
# (Adagio.ly, SonataIV.ly). Adagio 4 bars of 4/4; the Vivace is bars 5-65
# in 3/4, 61 bars; the tempo change falls one eighth into bar 5 (the
# pedal's B and the soprano's D-sharp still belong to the Adagio). No
# repeats in the score. The MIDI holds exactly these 65 bars.
#
# IMPORTANT, bar numbers in this file: midi2sng.py reads only the first
# time signature (4/4), so it counts the Vivace in 4/4 bars too. A score
# bar n of the Vivace (n >= 5) is position 5 + 0.75 * (n - 5) here.
# Score bar -> file position:
#   5 -> 5     9 -> 8      13 -> 11     16 -> 13.25   18 -> 14.75
#   25 -> 20   41 -> 32    43 -> 33.5   45 -> 35      52 -> 40.25
#   55 -> 42.5 60 -> 46.25 61 -> 47     65 -> 50 (the end is 50.75)
# A range "a-b" covers a up to b+1, so a range of score bars r1-r2
# is written P(r1) - (P(r2+1) - 1); those look odd but are exact.
# Also, `tempo` replaces every tempo in the MIDI, so the Adagio is made
# with `broaden` below.
#
# Tempo: Vivace quarter = 96, Adagio quarter = 32 (eighth = 64), about
# 2:25. Confirmed track times: George Baker (organ, 1976, Apple Music
# Classical) 2:22 for the whole movement; Roberto Loreggian and Mario
# Folena (harpsichord and flute arrangement) 2:33. Taking the Adagio at
# about 30 s, those give a Vivace of about quarter = 99 (Baker) and 90
# (Loreggian/Folena). Ton Koopman (Teldec, 1995) takes the whole sonata
# in 9:43 with the Andante at 4:38, so his outer movements together run
# 5:05; his split between them I could not confirm. The Mutopia MIDI
# has eighth = 68 and 186 (quarter 34 and 93). 96 sits in the middle:
# lively, and the sixteenths (9-10 frames each) stay clean on the chip.
# The Adagio tempo is my judgment, a little under the MIDI's.
#
# Structure (my reading of the score; the BWV 76 origin is from the
# Bach Vereniging's notes, the bar plan is mine):
#   1-4    Adagio, E minor; ends on the dominant, B major.
#   5-18   Vivace exposition: the subject (a long F-sharp falling into
#          sixteenths) in the alto at 5, the soprano at 9 a step behind
#          it at the same pitch; walking pedal; cadence in B minor at 18.
#   18-25  the pair again from C-sharp (alto 20), cadence B minor at 25.
#   25-35  soprano from F-sharp (25), alto from B (26); falling sequence
#          over a quaver pedal to C major and A minor; the soprano holds a
#          long E (31-33) while the alto takes the subject in A minor (31).
#   35-41  A minor, both upper lines in imitation; cadence A minor at 41.
#   41-51  E minor: the soprano's trilled D-sharp (42), the subject in
#          the soprano from B (43, 45), sequences on the pedal's eighths.
#   52-61  the alto's subject under a long soprano C (52); chromatic
#          approach and cadence in E minor at 61.
#   61-65  the soprano's last statement and the close in E minor.
#
# Plan: a trio is played on two manuals and pedal, one line to each, so
# an organist keeps one registration through a movement; at most an
# assistant adds or retires a stop at a main cadence. So the two upper
# lines are balanced equally (the voices cross often, and the chip ranks
# voices by pitch, so any difference would jump between them), the pedal
# keeps its full voice so its walking line reads, and the level changes
# only at the three main cadences. Pedal notes below B2 are lifted an
# octave by the converter; in the Vivace's walking bass this puts some
# notes into the alto's range, one more reason not to set the pedal back.

tempo 96              # Vivace, quarter
broaden 1-4.125 0.3333  # Adagio: quarter 32, up to the Vivace's entry
                        # one eighth into bar 5
broaden 4-4.125 0.9   # bar 4: hold back into the half cadence on B

voice top 0           # soprano and alto equal: two manuals of equal weight
voice mid 0
voice bottom 0        # the pedal at full voice

dyn 1 mp              # Adagio: grave and quiet
dyn 5.125 mf          # Vivace from its first quick note (score bar 5)
lead 8-10 top         # score 9-12: the soprano's first entry, above the
                      # alto's countersubject
broaden 13.25-13.75 0.95  # score 16-17: into the B minor cadence (bar 18)

dyn 20 mp             # score 25-40: after the second B minor cadence,
                      # the sequences and the A minor section
dyn 32 mf             # score 41-60: E minor, the soprano's entries
lead 33.5-34 top      # score 43-44: subject in the soprano from B
lead 40.25-41.5 mid   # score 52-54: the alto's subject under the held C
broaden 45.5-46 0.95    # score 59-60: into the E minor cadence (bar 61)

dyn 47 f              # score 61-65: the last statement and the close
rit 1.5 0.7           # score 64-65: the final cadence and held chord
